  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The start menu is the launcher collapsed to its GROUPS, with the group
 holding the selection showing its apps. Three things about it are decidable
 here and one of them is the defect a capture found on the first build.

 WHAT THE CAPTURE FOUND AND THIS PINS. The menu's first shape used `gpr-tree`
 whole, the flat list of fourteen entries in two columns, in a box anchored
 above the taskbar pill. It did not fit: at 1600x900 the Shutdown row landed
 past the bottom of the glass and the taskbar band was behind it. Nothing in
 the widget layer clips or scrolls and `flex-col-place` places a child past
 its container rather than refusing, so no count, no hit test and no id lookup
 could see it -- the tree reported every row reachable while a person could
 not see two of them. The row below measures the laid menu's BOTTOM against
 the band and is the arm that would have failed on that build.

 WHY THE HEADINGS CARRY A PREFIX, ASSERTED RATHER THAN STATED. `gpr-id-scan`
 matches a row id against `"gpr-" & label`; a heading's id is
 `gpr-group-prefix & group`. If the two namespaces ever overlapped, a heading
 would answer a scancode and clicking a group would launch an app. Both
 directions are asserted: a heading resolves through `gpr-group-scan` and NOT
 through `gpr-id-scan`, and a row the other way round. A single direction
 passes with either function answering for both.

 WHY THE COUNT IS TAKEN AT TWO SELECTIONS. A tree that ignored the selection
 and expanded everything would give the right ids, the right scancodes and a
 correct hit test; only the SHAPE would be wrong, and only against a second
 selection is the shape a claim rather than a number. The two counts differ
 because the groups differ in size, which is what makes them a discriminator:
 Accessories has four entries and Productivity six, so an expand-everything
 tree reports the same count for both and a collapse-everything tree reports
 the same smaller count for both.

 The face is `gfont-none` because the battery attaches no volume, which is the
 same reason the sibling arms in this directory use it (L-NAMED).

Section: Body

  dmg-yn : Boolean -> Text
  dmg-yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "NO"

  dmg-count : Integer -> Integer
  dmg-count (ps) =
    let t = gpr-menu-tree ps
    in t.wn-child-count

  dmg-sel : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dmg-sel (ps) (i) = let d = gpr-set-sel ps i in ps

  dmg-shape : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  dmg-shape (ps) (i) (tag) =
    let p = dmg-sel ps i
    in tag & " sel " & integer-to-text i
       & "  open " & gpr-open-group p
       & "  rows " & integer-to-text (dmg-count p)

 A heading names its group's FIRST entry, offset by one, so that zero can
 mean "not a heading". Selecting that entry is what opens the group, which is
 why the desk's step does exactly that with this answer.

  dmg-ids : Text -> Text
  dmg-ids (id) =
    "id " & id
       & "  group " & integer-to-text (gpr-group-scan id 0)
       & "  scancode " & integer-to-text (gpr-id-scan id 0)

 The laid menu, in LOGICAL units, against the band the taskbar lays out in the
 same pass and against the GLASS.

 THE GLASS IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND THE BAND IS NOT, WHICH THE SABOTAGE FOUND
 AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ARM GOT WRONG. Written as "the menu ends above
 the band" it reported yes under both shapes, because an over-tall menu does
 not overlap the band -- `flex-col-place` PUSHES the band down ahead of it and
 the two move together. Measured on the sabotaged build at 1600x900: the menu
 ran 58..480 and the band went with it to 488, so the comparison held while
 the band was 38 rows off the bottom of a 450-row screen. Two rectangles that
 move together cannot adjudicate each other; the container does not move.

 So the band-versus-menu row stays as a CALIBRATION and says so, and the row
 that fails is the band against the height the chrome was laid into. Both
 widths are needed and they do different jobs here: at 1280x800 the flat tree
 fitted, band at 764 inside 800, so that row is the control that must stay
 green while 1600 goes red. The bigger screen is the tighter one, as it has
 been for every clipped line this campaign found.

  dmg-laid : Integer, Integer, Integer -> WidgetNode
  dmg-laid (w) (h) (ds) =
    let s = ui-wscale w
    in widget-layout (desk-chrome-with (desk-menu-tree (peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell) gfont-none s) w ds) (dk-theme-ds ds) (layout-rect 0 0 (w / s) (h / s))

  dmg-box : WidgetNode, Text -> LayoutRect
  dmg-box (laid) (id) =
    when widget-find laid id
      is Just (n) -> n.wn-bounds
      is None -> layout-rect 0 0 0 0

  dmg-fits : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dmg-fits (w) (h) (ds) =
    let laid = dmg-laid w h ds
    in let menu = dmg-box laid "sysmenu-body"
    in let band = dmg-box laid "taskbar"
    in let glass = h / ui-wscale w
    in "w " & integer-to-text w
       & "  menu " & integer-to-text (menu.lr-y) & ".." & integer-to-text (menu.lr-y + menu.lr-h)
       & "  band " & integer-to-text (band.lr-y) & ".." & integer-to-text (band.lr-y + band.lr-h)
       & " of " & integer-to-text glass
       & "  band on the glass " & dmg-yn (band.lr-y + band.lr-h <= glass)
       & "  menu above the band (calibration) " & dmg-yn (menu.lr-y + menu.lr-h <= band.lr-y)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
    in let ap = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let pp = poke-32 ds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
    in let ps = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in act
      print-line-uni (dmg-shape ps 0 "accessories")
      print-line-uni (dmg-shape ps 4 "productivity")
      print-line-uni (dmg-ids (gpr-group-prefix & "Productivity"))
      print-line-uni (dmg-ids "gpr-Files")
      print-line-uni (dmg-ids "shutdown")
      print-line-uni (dmg-fits 1280 800 ds)
      print-line-uni (dmg-fits 1600 900 ds)
    end

